</div>
</div></section>

<nav class="home-topic-nav" aria-label="Explore the Lab model"><div class="page-width home-topic-links"><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#governance">Definitions <span aria-hidden="true">01</span></a><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#evidence">Evidence <span aria-hidden="true">02</span></a><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#agent">Work &amp; agents <span aria-hidden="true">03</span></a><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#change">Change <span aria-hidden="true">04</span></a></div></nav>

<section id="lab" class="page-width research-section section-grid" aria-labelledby="lab-title">
<p class="section-marker">01 / The company brain</p>
<div class="section-content"><h2 id="lab-title">Keep the knowledge<br>behind the work.</h2>
<div class="reading-copy"><p class="section-lead">A project carries more than tasks. It carries the meaning of its goals, the reasoning behind its decisions and the evidence of what happened.</p><p>The Lab gives that knowledge a continuing structure. Definitions state what important terms mean. Connected records show what was attempted, which evidence supports a claim and which decisions govern a change.</p><p>When work changes hands, the next person or agent has a place to recover those connections.</p></div>
<div class="home-model-map">{{COMPANY_BRAIN_MAP}}</div>
<figure class="brain-knowledge-cycle" aria-labelledby="knowledge-cycle-title"><h3 id="knowledge-cycle-title">How the connections become useful</h3><div class="knowledge-cycle-flow"><div class="knowledge-cycle-part cycle-knowledge"><span class="cycle-icon">{{BRAIN_KNOWLEDGE_ICON}}</span><strong>Knowledge</strong><p>Definitions, sources<br>and lessons</p></div><span class="knowledge-cycle-link">informs <b aria-hidden="true">→</b></span><div class="knowledge-cycle-part cycle-work"><span class="cycle-icon">{{BRAIN_WORK_ICON}}</span><strong>Work</strong><p>Plans, attempts<br>and evidence</p></div><span class="knowledge-cycle-link">supports review <b aria-hidden="true">→</b></span><div class="knowledge-cycle-part cycle-decisions"><span class="cycle-icon">{{BRAIN_DECISIONS_ICON}}</span><strong>Decisions</strong><p>Review, acceptance<br>and next steps</p></div></div><div class="knowledge-cycle-return"><span aria-hidden="true">↶</span> Accepted decisions govern changes to shared project state.</div><figcaption>Each attempt leaves evidence to examine. An accepted change follows its own authority route.</figcaption></figure>
</div></section>

<section id="how" class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="how-title"><div class="page-width section-grid">
<p class="section-marker">02 / How it works</p>
<div class="section-content"><h2 id="how-title">From an intention<br>to an accountable next step.</h2>
<ol class="product-steps"><li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span><div><h3>State what the work means.</h3><p>Express the intention and define the terms that determine success. “Complete” needs a criterion that everyone can examine.</p><a class="text-link" href="governance-lab-reference.html#governance">Definitions and authority <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></li><li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span><div><h3>Connect the work to its evidence.</h3><p>Identify the sources, methods and observations behind an assessment. Keep a finding connected to what supports it.</p><a class="text-link" href="governance-lab-reference.html#evidence">Provenance and evidence <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></li><li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div><h3>Make change explicit.</h3><p>A proposal, an accepted change and a deployment have different effects. Each follows its declared rules and authority.</p><a class="text-link" href="governance-lab-reference.html#change">Lifecycle and change <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></li><li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div><h3>Continue from usable context.</h3><p>Give the next session the relevant project knowledge. Check that its context and permissions still apply before continuing.</p><a class="text-link" href="governance-lab-reference.html#agent">Agent work and context <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></li></ol>
</div></div></section>

<section id="continuity" class="page-width research-section section-grid" aria-labelledby="continuity-title">
<p class="section-marker">03 / Working with agents</p>
<div class="section-content"><h2 id="continuity-title">The project remembers.<br>The agent continues.</h2><div class="reading-copy"><p class="section-lead">An agent session is temporary. The knowledge needed to continue the work belongs to the project.</p><p>The Lab organizes that durable knowledge as project state. Context views select the information needed for a task; ontology views show relevant concepts and their declared connections. A new session recovers its context from those views.</p><p>Short-term memory belongs to the session. If a view expires or permission no longer applies, the agent refreshes its context or pauses. Persisting a lesson also creates something to examine: later work needs evidence of its use and benefit.</p></div>
<figure class="session-handoff"><div><small>Session A</small><strong>Investigate &amp; record</strong><span>Temporary session memory</span></div><div class="handoff-state"><small>Project state</small><strong>Continuing knowledge</strong><span>Work, evidence &amp; decisions</span></div><div><small>Session B</small><strong>Recover &amp; continue</strong><span>Fresh views &amp; applicable permission</span></div><figcaption>A handoff between sessions. The recorded project knowledge provides continuity.</figcaption></figure>
</div></section>

<section id="craft" class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="craft-title"><div class="page-width section-grid">
<p class="section-marker">04 / Software work</p>
<div class="section-content"><h2 id="craft-title">Meet Craft</h2><div class="reading-copy"><p class="section-lead">Craft is the software-design product within the Lab. Its explicit models describe the concepts, rules and behavior an implementation must preserve.</p><p>The Lab keeps the project context, evidence and decisions around that work. A model’s capacity check, for example, evaluates a request through a Calculation. Reserving the capacity changes state through an Operation. Those separate responsibilities give the implementation a clear reference.</p></div><a class="ds-button" href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html">Read the Craft model <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
</div></section>

<section id="explore" class="page-width research-section section-grid explore-section" aria-labelledby="explore-title">
<p class="section-marker">05 / Explore the Lab</p>
<div class="section-content"><h2 id="explore-title">See the system<br>from the inside.</h2><div class="work-links"><a href="start-here.html"><span><small>Begin here</small><strong>Get to know CyberAlchemy Lab</strong><span>A short introduction to the complete model.</span></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a href="incident-walkthrough.html"><span><small>A concrete example</small><strong>Follow an incident</strong><span>See definitions, evidence, permissions and changes fit together.</span></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a href="governance-lab-reference.html#system-map"><span><small>A visual explanation</small><strong>Explore the system map</strong><span>Inspect the model through different lenses.</span></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a href="company-brain.html"><span><small>The product story</small><strong>Why we are building a company brain</strong><span>The problem, the approach and its connection to alignment.</span></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div>
<p class="product-origin">Built by CyberAlchemy, a research lab investigating intelligence and how people and machines work together. <a href="research.html">Our research, culture and ambition <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
</div></section>
</main>
